1240:\begin{lemma}[Restricted line distributions and error inflation]
1241:	\label{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds}
1242:	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.linePointDist_eq_mixture_restricted}
1243:	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.avg_restricted_le, MIPStarRE.QPBT.avg_restricted_prod_le}
1244:	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.restricted_lines_consistency_bound}
1245:	\leanok
1246:	% Source: unlabelled prose in the paper --- the discussion following
1247:	% def:ith-restricted-line at lines 1049-1061 of
1248:	% references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex.
1249:	\uses{def:ith-restricted-line, def:line-point-dist, lem:qld-xz-lines,
1250:		lem:qld-4-10, def:expanded-state}
1251:	The distribution $D_{\mathrm{ALine}}$ is a uniform mixture of the
1252:	distributions $D_{\mathrm{ALine},i}$ for $i \in \{1, \dots, m\}$, and
1253:	likewise $D_{\mathrm{DLine}}$ is a uniform mixture of the
1254:	$D_{\mathrm{DLine},i}$. As a consequence:
1255:	\begin{enumerate}
1256:		\item any approximation that holds on average over the line-point
1257:		distribution $D_{\mathrm{Line}}$ with error $\delta$ holds over each
1258:		restricted distribution $D_{\mathrm{ALine},i}$ and
1259:		$D_{\mathrm{DLine},i}$ with error $2m\delta$;
1260:		\item any approximation that holds on average over
1261:		$D_{\mathrm{Line}} \times D_{\mathrm{Line}}$ holds over any product
1262:		of restricted distributions with error $4m^2\delta$;
1263:		\item in particular, by Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines}, for any types
1264:		$\mathrm{v}_1, \mathrm{v}_2 \in \{\mathrm{ALine},
1265:		\mathrm{DLine}\}$ and any $i, j \in \{1, \dots, m\}$,
1266:		\begin{equation}
1267:			\label{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}
1268:			\E_{(\ell_X,x) \sim D_{\mathrm{v}_1,i}}\
1269:			\E_{(\ell_Z,z) \sim D_{\mathrm{v}_2,j}}
1270:			\sum_{f_X,f_Z}
1271:			\bra{\hat\psi}
1272:			\big(T^{\ell_X,\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\big)_{\mathsf{A}\mathsf{A}'}
1273:			\ot
1274:			\big(\Id - \hat{Q}^{x,z}_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}
1275:			\big)_{\mathsf{B}\mathsf{A}''}
1276:			\ket{\hat\psi}
1277:			= O(m^2 \delta_P)\,.
1278:		\end{equation}
1279:	\end{enumerate}
1280:\end{lemma}
1281:\begin{proof}
1282:	\leanok
1283:	\uses{lem:qld-xz-lines, def:line-point-dist, def:consistency}
1284:	The line-point distribution places weight $\tfrac{1}{2}$ on each of the
1285:	axis-parallel and diagonal components and, within each component, uniform
1286:	weight on the index $i$, so each restricted distribution carries mixture
1287:	weight $\tfrac{1}{2m}$ in $D_{\mathrm{Line}}$. A nonnegative average that
1288:	is at most $\delta$ therefore bounds each mixture component by
1289:	$2m\delta$, and products of two such mixtures give the factor
1290:	$(2m)^2 = 4m^2$. The instance \eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted} is item
1291:	2 applied to the conclusion of Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines}, whose
1292:	$\simeq_{\delta_P}$ relation over two independent copies of the
1293:	line-point distribution is precisely the statement that the left-hand
1294:	side of \eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}, averaged over
1295:	$D_{\mathrm{Line}} \times D_{\mathrm{Line}}$, is at most $\delta_P$.
1296:\end{proof}
